**Terraform, for all of it** — not raw `az` commands. Let me explain why, since this connects to lessons you've already learned the hard way this project.

## The reasoning

Every time you've applied something via CLI instead of Terraform, it created drift, and every drift incident this project has cost you real time (the TL's manual portal change, the image-tag mismatch, the frontend env vars set in the portal). You know this pattern now — **CLI changes are invisible to Terraform until someone syncs the code**, and that gap is where problems hide.

For genuine one-time bootstrapping problems (the remote-state chicken-and-egg, the frontend Key Vault access deadlock), CLI was the right call because Terraform literally couldn't do it yet. **None of today's security changes have that excuse** — CORS, ingress, and Cosmos network rules are all things Terraform can express directly and cleanly.

## What each change looks like in Terraform

**CORS** — already a plain `env` block value; just edit the string in `main.tf` (as I gave you earlier).

**Backend internal-only ingress** — the `external_enabled` attribute inside the `ingress` block of the `azurerm_container_app` resource. Also natively supported.

**Cosmos network restriction** — the `azurerm_cosmosdb_account` resource supports `is_virtual_network_filter_enabled`, `network_acl_bypass_for_azure_services`, and `ip_range_filter` as first-class arguments. No need for a separate `az cosmosdb update` command.

## The one exception — verification only

The **only** CLI commands you should run are read-only ones, to *check* things before or after a Terraform change:
```
az containerapp show --name primaryddev-frontend ... --query "..." -o tsv
```

(to get the exact frontend FQDN for CORS)
```
az containerapp show --name primaryddev-backend ... --query "properties.configuration.ingress" -o json
```
(to verify ingress changed after apply)

These **read** Azure's state to inform or confirm — they never **change** anything. That distinction (read vs. write) is the line: reads are always safe outside Terraform, writes should always go through Terraform for anything that isn't a one-time bootstrap problem.

## The complete Terraform-only sequence

```
git checkout dev
git pull origin dev
git checkout -b feature/security-hardening
```

**Get the exact frontend URL first (read-only, informs the edit):**
```
az containerapp show --name primaryddev-frontend --resource-group GENAI-B5-Project-T3-RG --query "properties.configuration.ingress.fqdn" -o tsv
```

**One combined prompt for all three changes:**
```
Make three changes in infra/modules/container_apps/main.tf and 
infra/modules/cosmos/main.tf:

1. In the BACKEND azurerm_container_app's env blocks, change 
   CORS_ORIGINS value from "*" to 
   "https://<exact-frontend-fqdn-from-az-command>"

2. In the BACKEND azurerm_container_app's ingress block, change 
   external_enabled from true to false.

3. In infra/modules/cosmos/main.tf, on the azurerm_cosmosdb_account 
   resource, add network_acl_bypass_for_azure_services = true (or the 
   correct current attribute name for allowing Azure-service access - 
   verify against current azurerm provider docs).

Also add an output backend_internal_fqdn to 
infra/modules/container_apps/outputs.tf pointing at 
azurerm_container_app.backend.ingress[0].fqdn.

Then update the FRONTEND container's BACKEND_BASE_URL env value to 
reference that internal fqdn output instead of the hardcoded public URL.

Show me all changed files.
```

**Then the careful plan/apply cycle:**
```
cd infra\envs\dev
terraform plan
```
Read it fully — confirm backend ingress flips, frontend's `BACKEND_BASE_URL` updates, Cosmos gets the network setting, nothing destroyed.
```
terraform apply
```

**Then verify (read-only CLI, checking the result):**
```
az containerapp show --name primaryddev-backend --resource-group GENAI-B5-Project-T3-RG --query "properties.configuration.ingress.external" -o tsv
```
Should now show `false`.

Open the frontend URL in a browser, confirm the app still works end-to-end.

**Then commit:**
```
cd ../../..
git add infra/
git commit -m "Security hardening: restrict CORS, backend internal-only ingress, Cosmos network restriction"
git push -u origin feature/security-hardening
```
→ PR to dev, with the security-notes doc included.

So: Terraform for every actual change, CLI only to read/verify. Run the frontend-FQDN lookup first and tell me what it returns — then we finalize the exact CORS value before running the combined prompt.
